{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_2_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_2_guesses": "never", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "Easy Part: There were two \"freebies\" of (1, 8) in two columns. This made starting the puzzle a lot easier as I could \"X\" many adjacent squares\n\nDifficult: Endgame was hard. ", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "Hard: Less obvious freebies like (1, 8), harder to start game. Had to consider many rows/columns so I couldn't just right away fill out an entire row or column. \n\nEasy: There was a column that was just \"4\". This gave the game away. There were a couple of rows that were like (1, 2, 1, 1) which gives you very limited options. ", "strategy": "1. Fill out freebies and eliminate any adjacent cells that don't work \n2. Look at the start and end of the sequences. This can often eliminate or fill out the start or end. \n3. Overlap of potential positions. For example, (1, 4, 1), 4 itself was very limited to where it could have been placed. Look for where a piece of 4 is guaranteed to fill. ", "comments": "I liked it :D Best of luck with the research!\n\n"}, "t": "2026-05-25T22:07:49.413949+00:00"}
